{
  "id": "bata_sango_oyo",
  "name": "Ìlù bàtá (Yoruba bàtá drumming for Ṣàngó)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Drumming and song of the Yoruba bàtá ensemble, the drum family sacred to Ṣàngó, the orìṣà of thunder and deified king of Ọ̀yọ́, and also played for Egúngún masquerades. A set of conical double-headed bàtá drums led by the ìyá ìlù mother drum renders praise texts in drum speech alongside sung praise for the orìṣà. William Bascom's field recordings of Yoruba drumming, made around 1951, were issued on the Folkways LP Drums of the Yoruba of Nigeria. The bata card stands in for the Nigerian bàtá set.",
  "recipe": "classic early-1950s ìlù bàtá, ritual Devotional, indigenous branch. tonal language syllable tone locked melody. sacred bata. field recording. small-diaphragm pencil condenser, portable field-recorder pre. tape 15ips.",
